.TH OPCCHECK 1 "January 2024" "opccheck 2.0.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
opccheck \- comprehensive OPC-DA security checker for industrial control systems
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B opccheck
[\fIOPTIONS\fR] \fITARGET\fR
.br
.B opccheck
\-\-list\-checks
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B opccheck
is a comprehensive command-line tool for security assessment of OPC-DA (OLE for Process Control \- Data Access) servers and related industrial protocols.
It performs extensive security checks including DCOM connectivity tests, OPC interface accessibility verification, authentication testing, and more.
.PP
The tool supports multiple OPC specifications including:
.IP \(bu 2
OPC-DA (Data Access) versions 1.0, 2.0, and 3.0
.IP \(bu 2
OPC-HDA (Historical Data Access)
.IP \(bu 2
OPC-AE (Alarms & Events)
.IP \(bu 2
OPC Batch
.IP \(bu 2
OPC Security specification
.PP
OPC-DA is widely used in industrial control systems (ICS), SCADA systems, and manufacturing environments.
This tool helps identify security misconfigurations that could expose industrial systems to unauthorized access.
.PP
The tool is designed to work on Linux and macOS systems, implementing DCOM/RPC protocol handling without requiring Windows dependencies.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS "Target Specification"
.TP
.I TARGET
The target OPC-DA server specified as a hostname, DNS name, or IP address.
Optional when using \-\-list\-checks.
.SS "Port Scanning Options"
.TP
.B \-\-scan\-ports
Scan for accessible DCOM ports. This is enabled by default.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-port\-scan
Disable port scanning.
.TP
.BI \-\-port\-range " START-END"
Specify a custom port range to scan (e.g., 49152-49200).
.TP
.B \-\-common\-ports\-only
Only scan commonly used DCOM ports. This is the default behavior.
.TP
.BI \-p " PORT" "\fR,\fP \-\-port " PORT
Check a specific port only.
.SS "Check Selection Options"
.TP
.B \-\-list\-checks
List all available security checks organized by category and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-check\-all
Enable all security checks including optional ones (OPC-HDA, OPC-AE, Windows services, etc.).
.TP
.BI \-\-enable\-check " CHECK"
Enable a specific check by its ID. Can be used multiple times.
Example: \-\-enable\-check opc_hda \-\-enable\-check opc_ae
.TP
.BI \-\-disable\-check " CHECK"
Disable a specific check by its ID. Can be used multiple times.
Example: \-\-disable\-check null_session
.TP
.BI \-\-only\-checks " CHECKS"
Run only the specified checks. Provide a comma-separated list of check IDs.
Example: \-\-only\-checks authentication,browsing,sync_io
.SS "OPC-Specific Options"
.TP
.B \-\-check\-hda
Enable OPC-HDA (Historical Data Access) interface checks.
Tests IOPCHDA_Server, IOPCHDA_Browser, IOPCHDA_SyncRead, and IOPCHDA_SyncUpdate interfaces.
.TP
.B \-\-check\-ae
Enable OPC-AE (Alarms & Events) interface checks.
Tests IOPCEventServer, IOPCEventSubscriptionMgt, and IOPCEventAreaBrowser interfaces.
.TP
.B \-\-check\-batch
Enable OPC Batch interface checks.
Tests IOPCBatchServer and IOPCEnumerationSets interfaces.
.TP
.B \-\-check\-windows
Enable Windows service enumeration checks.
Tests WMI, SAMR, LSA, and SRVSVC interfaces.
.SS "Output Options"
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Enable verbose output with detailed information about each check.
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Quiet mode \- only display security findings, suppress progress messages.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Output results in JSON format for integration with other tools.
.TP
.B \-\-xml
Output results in XML format.
.TP
.B \-\-csv
Output results in CSV format.
.TP
.BI \-o " FILE" "\fR,\fP \-\-output " FILE
Write results to the specified file in JSON format.
.TP
.BI \-\-min\-severity " LEVEL"
Minimum severity level to display. Valid values: INFO, LOW, MEDIUM, HIGH, CRITICAL.
Default is INFO (show all results).
.TP
.B \-\-no\-color
Disable colored output in terminal.
.TP
.B \-\-show\-passed
Show passed checks in output. This is the default.
.TP
.B \-\-hide\-passed
Hide passed checks, only show security findings (failed checks).
.SS "Connection Options"
.TP
.BI \-t " SECONDS" "\fR,\fP \-\-timeout " SECONDS
Set connection timeout in seconds. Default is 5 seconds.
.TP
.BI \-\-retry " COUNT"
Number of retries for failed connections. Default is 1 (no retry).
.TP
.BI \-\-delay " SECONDS"
Delay between checks in seconds. Default is 0 (no delay).
Useful for avoiding rate limiting.
.TP
.BI \-\-source\-ip " IP"
Source IP address to use for outgoing connections.
.SS "General Options"
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Display version information and exit.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Display help message and exit.
.SH AVAILABLE CHECKS
Use \fB\-\-list\-checks\fR to see all available security checks.
Checks are organized by category:
.SS "Network & Ports"
.TP
.B dcom_ports
DCOM Port Accessibility (default: enabled)
.TP
.B endpoint_mapper
RPC Endpoint Mapper (default: enabled)
.TP
.B smb_signing
SMB Signing (default: disabled)
.TP
.B connection_limits
Connection rate limiting detection (default: disabled)
.SS "Authentication"
.TP
.B authentication
OPC-DA Authentication requirements (default: enabled)
.TP
.B ntlm_auth
NTLM Authentication support (default: enabled)
.TP
.B null_session
Null Session (anonymous) Access (default: enabled)
.SS "OPC-DA Interfaces"
.TP
.B browsing
OPC Item Browsing via IOPCBrowseServerAddressSpace (default: enabled)
.TP
.B server_enumeration
OPC Server Enumeration via IOPCServerList (default: enabled)
.TP
.B da_version
OPC-DA Version Detection (1.0, 2.0, 3.0) (default: enabled)
.TP
.B sync_io
Synchronous I/O Access via IOPCSyncIO (default: enabled)
.TP
.B async_io
Asynchronous I/O Access via IOPCAsyncIO2 (default: enabled)
.TP
.B item_management
Item Management via IOPCItemMgt (default: enabled)
.TP
.B group_management
Group Management via IOPCGroupStateMgt (default: enabled)
.TP
.B item_properties
Item Properties via IOPCItemProperties (default: enabled)
.TP
.B callback_interface
Data Callback via IOPCDataCallback (default: enabled)
.TP
.B item_deadband
Item Deadband Management (default: disabled)
.TP
.B public_groups
Public Groups Access (default: disabled)
.SS "DCOM Services"
.TP
.B remote_activation
DCOM Remote Activation via OXID Resolver (default: enabled)
.SS "Security Interfaces"
.TP
.B opc_security
OPC Security Interface (IOPCSecurityNT, IOPCSecurityPrivate) (default: enabled)
.SS "Other OPC Specifications"
.TP
.B opc_hda
OPC-HDA (Historical Data Access) interfaces (default: disabled)
.TP
.B opc_ae
OPC-AE (Alarms & Events) interfaces (default: disabled)
.TP
.B opc_batch
OPC Batch interfaces (default: disabled)
.TP
.B opc_dx
OPC-DX (Data eXchange) interfaces (default: disabled)
.TP
.B opc_commands
OPC Commands interfaces (default: disabled)
.SS "Windows Services"
.TP
.B wmi_dcom
WMI over DCOM (default: disabled)
.TP
.B samr_access
SAM-R Protocol Access for user enumeration (default: disabled)
.TP
.B lsa_access
LSA Protocol Access for security policies (default: disabled)
.TP
.B srvsvc_access
Server Service Access for share enumeration (default: disabled)
.SH SEVERITY LEVELS
Results are classified by severity:
.TP
.B CRITICAL
Issues requiring immediate attention. Anonymous access or critical misconfigurations.
.TP
.B HIGH
Significant security concerns that should be addressed promptly.
Includes accessible I/O interfaces, remote activation, and null session access.
.TP
.B MEDIUM
Moderate security issues that increase attack surface.
Includes accessible browsing, enumeration, and management interfaces.
.TP
.B LOW
Minor security concerns or best practice violations.
.TP
.B INFO
Informational findings with no direct security impact.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
No critical or high severity issues found.
.TP
.B 1
High severity issues found.
.TP
.B 2
Critical severity issues found.
.TP
.B 130
Operation interrupted by user (Ctrl+C).
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Basic security check:
.B opccheck 192.168.1.100
.TP
Verbose check with DNS hostname:
.B opccheck opcserver.example.com \-v
.TP
Run all available checks:
.B opccheck 10.0.0.50 \-\-check\-all
.TP
Enable specific checks:
.B opccheck 192.168.1.100 \-\-enable\-check opc_hda \-\-enable\-check opc_ae
.TP
Disable specific checks:
.B opccheck 192.168.1.100 \-\-disable\-check null_session
.TP
Run only specific checks:
.B opccheck 192.168.1.100 \-\-only\-checks authentication,browsing
.TP
Extended port scan with custom range:
.B opccheck 10.0.0.50 \-\-port\-range 49152-49200
.TP
Skip port scanning:
.B opccheck 192.168.1.100 \-\-no\-port\-scan
.TP
Extended timeout with retries:
.B opccheck server.local \-\-timeout 10 \-\-retry 3
.TP
JSON output to file:
.B opccheck 192.168.1.100 \-\-json \-o results.json
.TP
Show only HIGH and CRITICAL findings:
.B opccheck 192.168.1.100 \-\-min\-severity HIGH
.TP
Hide passed checks:
.B opccheck 192.168.1.100 \-\-hide\-passed
.TP
Check specific port:
.B opccheck 192.168.1.100 \-p 135
.TP
Enable OPC-HDA and OPC-AE checks:
.B opccheck 192.168.1.100 \-\-check\-hda \-\-check\-ae
.TP
Enable Windows service checks:
.B opccheck 192.168.1.100 \-\-check\-windows
.TP
List all available checks:
.B opccheck \-\-list\-checks
.TP
XML output:
.B opccheck 192.168.1.100 \-\-xml
.TP
CSV output:
.B opccheck 192.168.1.100 \-\-csv
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/local/bin/opccheck
Default installation location.
.TP
.I ~/.opccheck/config
User configuration file (optional, not yet implemented).
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B OPCCHECK_TIMEOUT
Default timeout value in seconds. Overridden by \-\-timeout option.
.SH SECURITY CONSIDERATIONS
.B Important:
This tool is intended for authorized security testing and research only.
Always obtain proper authorization before testing systems you do not own.
.PP
Recommended use cases include:
.IP \(bu 2
Security assessments of industrial control systems
.IP \(bu 2
Authorized penetration testing of OPC infrastructure
.IP \(bu 2
Compliance verification for OPC-DA deployments
.IP \(bu 2
Network security audits
.IP \(bu 2
Vulnerability assessments
.SH NOTES
.SS "OPC-DA Versions"
The tool can detect and test interfaces specific to different OPC-DA versions:
.IP \(bu 2
\fBDA 1.0\fR: Basic OPC functionality with IOPCAsyncIO
.IP \(bu 2
\fBDA 2.0\fR: Enhanced functionality with IOPCAsyncIO2
.IP \(bu 2
\fBDA 3.0\fR: Latest version with IOPCAsyncIO3 and IOPCBrowse
.SS "OPC-HDA"
OPC Historical Data Access allows reading and potentially writing historical process data.
The IOPCHDA_SyncUpdate interface is particularly sensitive as it may allow modification of historical records.
.SS "OPC-AE"
OPC Alarms & Events provides access to alarm conditions and system events.
Access to these interfaces may allow an attacker to suppress alarms or miss critical events.
.SS "Null Sessions"
Null session access via SAMR, LSA, or SRVSVC can allow:
.IP \(bu 2
Enumeration of users and groups
.IP \(bu 2
Discovery of security policies
.IP \(bu 2
Enumeration of network shares
